There is a logical bug in how permissions are checked. This method checks for a resource specific to the client, and only if it doesn't exist (`resource == null`), it falls back to checking the resource for the resource type (i.e., 'all clients').

This is incorrect. A user should be granted permission if they have it either on the specific client resource OR on the 'all clients' resource type. For example, a user with a 'view-all-clients' permission should be able to view a specific client even if that client has a resource object with other permissions defined for other users.

The current logic will incorrectly deny access in such scenarios.

To fix this, you should check for permissions on both the specific client resource (if it exists) and the resource type, and grant access if either check passes. A good approach would be to check the type-level permission first, and if that doesn't grant access, then check the specific resource permission.

Suggested change:
```java
    private boolean hasPermission(ClientModel client, String scope) {
        // First, check for a broad permission on the resource type (e.g., 'manage-all-clients')
        if (hasPermission(scope)) {
            return true;
        }

        if (!root.isAdminSameRealm()) {
            return false;
        }
        ResourceServer server = root.realmResourceServer();
        if (server == null) return false;

        // If no broad permission, check for a specific permission on the client resource
        Resource resource =  resourceStore.findByName(server, client.getId(), server.getId());
        if (resource == null) {
            return false;
        }

        Collection<Permission> permissions = root.evaluatePermission(new ResourcePermission(resource, resource.getScopes(), server), server);
        List<String> expectedScopes = Arrays.asList(scope);
        for (Permission permission : permissions) {
            for (String s : permission.getScopes()) {
                if (expectedScopes.contains(s)) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
```
